        "original_id": "4bf6cf53-f7b5-501e-9e6f-5a9bb100c5b3",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Max Ehrmann, Les Crane\n- Works: Desiderata\n- Places: Terre Haute, Indiana\n- Events: 1971 and 1972 recordings, Grammy award\n\nFacts:\n- Max Ehrmann was born on September 26, 1872, and he was an American writer, poet, and attorney from Terre Haute, Indiana.\n- Max Ehrmann wrote the 1927 prose poem \"Desiderata,\" and the Latin title means \"things desired\" or \"desired things.\"\n- \"Desiderata\" was largely unknown during Max Ehrmann's lifetime.\n- Les Crane was born on December 3, 1933, and he was a radio announcer and television talk show host who pioneered interactive broadcasting.\n- \"Desiderata\" became widely known after devotional and spoken-word recordings used the text in 1971 and 1972.\n- Les Crane scored a hit with his 1971 spoken-word recording of \"Desiderata\" and won a Grammy for Best Spoken Word.\n- Max Ehrmann died on September 9, 1945.\n- Les Crane died on July 13, 2008.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
